  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A benefit-risk trial of 1-month rifapentine and isoniazid to prevent tuberculosis and reduce morbidity in people with non-communicable multimorbidity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MRC AGHRB Award to define the best pragmatic strategy for TB prevention in a large neglected population of HIV uninfected individuals with diabetes mellitus (diabetes) who are at risk of TB in Asia and Africa</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Our overall objective is to define the best pragmatic strategy for TB prevention in a large neglected population of HIVuninfected individuals with diabetes mellitus (diabetes) who are at risk of TB in Asia and Africa. The value of TB preventive
therapy in addition to the standard of care for diabetes as well as the benefit-risk will be assessed in a randomized controlled implementation strategy trial. Effective TB control in this population is expected to yield morbidity and mortality
benefits through improved outcomes of these overlapping chronic conditions whilst also being cost-saving. Underpinning activities will inform implementation and address specific capacity development needs in project countries.
This project explores the unique bidirectional association between TB and diabetes through the following specific objectives:
Primary objective:
a. To evaluate the effectiveness of the ultra-short-course TB preventive treatment (TPT) with 1-month daily rifapentine plus isoniazid (1HP) compared to no TPT in people with diabetes prescribed WHO standard of care for diabetes (WHO SoC),
with respect to the risk of microbiologically confirmed TB disease in 2 years. Hypothesis: Compared to WHO SoC without 1HP, WHO SoC with 1HP has superior effectiveness for prevention of TB among HIV-uninfected people with diabetes who have latent TB infection (indicating immunosensitization to Mycobacterium tuberculosis).
Secondary objectives:
a. To evaluate the safety and tolerability of 1HP vs WHO SoC for diabetes without 1HP with respect to the risk of prespecified grade &gt;=3 adverse events (AE), impact of increased pill burden on adherence of usual medication or treatment completion of 1HP, as well as worsening or exacerbation of the chronic condition during follow up.
b. To evaluate alternate effectiveness outcomes: 1) Probable, possible or definite diagnoses of TB disease or all deaths (excluding violent and accidental deaths) (2) Disease-specific deaths (TB and NCD-related) , (3) Durability of the TB protective effect (until study end; up to 4 years).
c. To determine the clinical benefit-risk trade-off of 1HP for TB prevention in people with diabetes prescribed WHO SoC for diabetes compared to standard care without 1HP.
d. To identify subgroups of people with diabetes that most benefit from 1HP
e. To explore the acceptability and feasibility of TB preventive therapy among patients and examine the potential for integration of the intervention within existing health systems.
f. To estimate the cost and cost-effectiveness of 1HP in addition to WHO SoC for diabetes vs WHO SoC for diabetes alone from health system and societal perspectives. Substudies to evaluate under separate protocols and funding from Wellcome Trust: a. Drug-drug interactions, to determine inducing/inhibiting effect of co-administration on concentrations of medications used in usual care for diabetes or between study drugs, stratified by isoniazid metabolizer genotypes.
b. Immunological mechanisms of benefit, particularly in diabetics prescribed metformin (a newly recognized host directed therapy for TB)</narrative>
  </description>
